---
import Article from '../layouts/Article.astro';
import ThermostatCalc from '../components/ThermostatCalc.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';

const toc = [
  { id: 'temperature', label: 'Set the right temperature' },
  { id: 'filters', label: 'Keep the filters clean' },
  { id: 'features', label: 'Use your system’s features' },
  { id: 'home', label: 'Make smart choices at home' },
  { id: 'replace', label: 'Change over an old unit' },
  { id: 'build', label: 'Planning to build?' },
];
---

<Article
  title="How to Reduce Air Conditioning Running Costs"
  description="Simple ways to cut your air conditioning running costs: the right thermostat settings, clean filters, zoning, timers and smart choices around your home."
  crumb="Reduce running costs"
  heading="How to reduce your air conditioning running costs."
  lede="When summer and winter arrive, so do bigger energy bills. These simple tips will help keep your running costs down during the busiest months."
  photo="remoteControl"
  toc={toc}
>
  <h2 id="temperature">Set your air conditioner to the right temperature</h2>
  <p>
    Running your air conditioner too cold in summer, or too warm in winter, pushes up your energy bills and adds wear and tear
    to the motor. We recommend <strong>24–26°C in summer</strong> and <strong>19–21°C in winter</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    In summer, every degree cooler than that range adds roughly 10% to your running costs, and in winter every degree warmer
    adds about 10%. The less the compressor has to work, the lower your bills.
  </p>

  <ThermostatCalc id="costs-thermo" />

  <h2 id="filters">Keep your aircon’s filters clean</h2>
  <p>
    Dirt and dust clog the filters and reduce your system’s performance, so it has to work harder to keep your home at the
    right temperature. Cleaning them is a simple job that keeps your air conditioner running efficiently. See our
    <a href="/air-conditioning-maintenance-tips">maintenance tips</a> for how, and always follow the manufacturer’s
    recommendations.
  </p>

  <h2 id="features">Use the features on your system</h2>
  <p>
    Most reverse cycle systems have features that help them heat and cool more efficiently, such as timers, human sensors and
    zoning.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Zones:</strong> switching off unused zones on a ducted system lowers your energy use.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Timers:</strong> start the system before the outside temperature peaks. Turning it on in the hottest part of the
      day makes it work much harder to cool your home.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Human sensors:</strong> some systems switch to an energy-saving mode when no movement has been detected for 20
      minutes.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="home">Make smart choices around your home</h2>
  <p>Cutting your heating and cooling costs isn’t only about the air conditioner. Small changes reduce its workload too.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>With reverse cycle systems, keep doors and windows closed while the system is running.</li>
    <li>
      Use natural heating and cooling. In winter, open the curtains to let the sun in and close them at night to keep the
      warmth in. In summer, keep curtains drawn in direct sunlight and open windows at night if it’s cooler outside.
    </li>
    <li>Make sure your home is well insulated. Old insulation that has flattened out does very little and should be replaced.</li>
    <li>Curtains, roller blinds or shutters on large windows keep direct sunlight out.</li>
    <li>Turn off appliances you aren’t using. They use power and add heat, making your air conditioner work harder.</li>
    <li>Ceiling or portable fans help spread cool or warm air through your home faster.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="callout callout--blue">
    <Icon name="info" />
    <p>
      Evaporative coolers are the exception: they need ventilation, so open some windows and doors to let the air flow
      through the house.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="replace">Change over your old unit</h2>
  <p>
    An old ducted unit can be a real drain on your energy bills. Older units have to work harder and use less efficient
    technology. Replacing your existing ducted system with a new high-efficiency inverter will reduce your running costs. Find
    out more about <a href="/air-conditioning-adelaide/ducted-air-conditioning-replacement-units">ducted replacement units</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="build">Planning to build?</h2>
  <p>
    Smart house design makes a big difference, so keep energy efficiency in mind when you plan your build. The more efficient
    your house is, the less energy it takes to keep it comfortable, and the lower your bills will be.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Large glass windows add significantly to the heat load and need more air conditioning power, so curtains are a must.</li>
    <li>Plain silvery galvanised iron roofing reflects heat better than dark Colorbond, which absorbs it.</li>
    <li>If you have a roof space, consider ventilation such as a whirlybird to reduce heat build-up.</li>
    <li>If space permits, larger eaves or a verandah will reduce heat loads by blocking direct sunlight.</li>
  </ul>
</Article>
